DevOps Internship - Week 5 Report

Topic :  Jenkins, CI/CD & GitHub Integration

-> Introduction
Week 5 focuses on Jenkins, CI/CD and GitHub Integration. The learning areas include Jenkins
fundamentals and architecture, installation and configuration, jobs and builds, credentials, GitHub
integration, Freestyle Projects, Declarative Pipelines, Jenkinsfile, pipeline stages, build triggers, GitHub
Webhooks, build and test automation, troubleshooting and CI/CD best practices. The learning approach
emphasizes understanding how code moves through a CI/CD pipeline and using AI as an assistant
rather than a replacement for understanding.

-> Objectives
The objectives of this week were to understand Jenkins and CI/CD concepts, install and configure
Jenkins, connect Jenkins with GitHub, configure credentials securely, create Jenkins jobs and pipelines,
automate builds, analyze build logs and understand how webhooks can trigger automated workflows.

-> What is CI/CD?
CI/CD is a core DevOps practice that automates software delivery. In a CI pipeline, code changes are
validated continuously through automated stages such as checkout, build and testing. Jenkins is used to
help teams build, test and validate applications continuously.

-> Jenkins Architecture
Jenkins is an automation server used to create and execute build and CI/CD workflows. Users configure
jobs or pipelines through Jenkins, and Jenkins executes the configured stages. During troubleshooting,
Jenkins Console Output is useful for understanding why a build failed.

-> Jenkins Installation and Configuration
The weekly task requires installing and configuring Jenkins and exploring the Jenkins Dashboard. After
installation, Jenkins should be configured for the required jobs and pipeline activities.

-> Jenkins Jobs and Freestyle Projects
A Jenkins job defines work that Jenkins should perform. The weekly task requires creating a Freestyle
Project that pulls application code from GitHub. Freestyle Projects provide a simple way to configure
source code, build steps and triggers through the Jenkins interface.

-> Jenkins Credentials
Jenkins Credentials are used to securely manage information required for integrations and access. The
weekly task specifically requires configuring Jenkins Credentials securely before connecting Jenkins
workflows with external services such as GitHub.

-> GitHub Integration
Jenkins can be connected with a GitHub repository so that Jenkins can retrieve application code and
perform automated validation. The Week 5 task requires connecting Jenkins with GitHub and creating a
project that pulls code from GitHub.

-> Declarative Pipeline
A Declarative Pipeline defines an automated workflow using clearly organized stages. The weekly task
requires creating a Declarative Pipeline and executing it while analyzing the build logs.

-> Jenkinsfile
A Jenkinsfile stores the pipeline definition as code. The assignment requires writing a Jenkinsfile and
understanding every stage of the pipeline. This allows the CI workflow to be maintained together with the
application project.

-> Pipeline Stages
The weekly task requires pipeline stages for Checkout, Build and Test. The hands-on activity additionally
requires a Validation stage. Separating the pipeline into stages makes it easier to understand where a
failure occurs and to analyze the results.

-> GitHub Webhooks
A GitHub Webhook can notify Jenkins when changes occur in a repository. The weekly task requires
basic GitHub Webhook integration, while the hands-on activity requires pushing a new change and
verifying that Jenkins automatically starts the pipeline.

-> Hands-on CI Pipeline
The hands-on scenario is based on developers continuously pushing code to GitHub while a Junior
DevOps Engineer creates a CI pipeline to automatically validate every change. The required pipeline
stages are Checkout, Build, Test and Validation. The repository is connected to Jenkins and a GitHub
Webhook is configured to trigger automated pipeline execution.

-> Failure and Troubleshooting
The hands-on activity requires introducing a controlled error into the project, observing the failed Jenkins
build, analyzing the Console Output, identifying the issue, fixing it and running the pipeline again. The
complete failure, troubleshooting and resolution process should be documented.

-> Testing and Verification
The Jenkins installation and dashboard should be verified. GitHub integration and credentials should be
checked, and the pipeline should be executed through its stages. A GitHub change should trigger an
automated build through the configured webhook. Both failed and successful pipeline runs should be
observed, and the Console Output should be reviewed to identify the root cause of failures.

-> Conclusion
Week 5 provided practical exposure to Jenkins, CI/CD automation and GitHub integration. The work
demonstrated how code changes can move through automated pipeline stages and how Jenkins can
provide useful feedback when builds fail. The troubleshooting activity also emphasized reading logs,
identifying root causes, fixing issues and verifying the pipeline again. These activities support the
DevOps mindset of automating, troubleshooting and improving delivery workflows.